      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e1a0311b-5fde-5f66-a554-a115734154a4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: William N. Fenton\n- Organizations: Iroquois League, Iroquois Confederacy, Five Nations, Six Nations\n- Peoples: Mohawk, Onondaga, Oneida, Cayuga, Seneca, Tuscarora\n- Topics: Iroquois history and culture\n\nFacts:\n- William N. Fenton was born on December 15, 1908, and he became an American scholar and writer known for extensive studies of Iroquois history and culture.\n- During the colonial years, the French referred to the Iroquois as the Iroquois League and later as the Iroquois Confederacy.\n- Before 1722, the English referred to the Iroquois as the Five Nations, and later as the Six Nations.\n- The Six Nations comprised the Mohawk, Onondaga, Oneida, Cayuga, Seneca, and Tuscarora peoples.\n- William N. Fenton died on June 17, 2005.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
